WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:22.620
DON’T LOOK BACK:
On August 10, 2015, Larry Page and Sergey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.920
Brin made Sundar Pichai the CEO of Google. At&nbsp;
first glance, it seemed like the duo just wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.960
to play a more hands off role at the company.&nbsp;
While Sundar handled all of the day to day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.720
nuances of running Google, Larry and Sergey could&nbsp;
focus on the long term future of the company. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:46.260
just 4 years later in December of 2019, Larry&nbsp;
and Sergey would make Sundar the CEO of Alphabet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:52.560
itself and they would more or less fall of the&nbsp;
grid completely. They rarely make any public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.560
appearances and they’re notorious for skipping&nbsp;
meetings. This is quite out of the blue given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.440
that most founders tend to play a significant role&nbsp;
in their companies until the day they die. I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:10.140
just look at Warren Buffett or Churlie Munger.&nbsp;
Larry and Sergey, however, have truly left the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:17.160
company and the spotlight despite still having 51%&nbsp;
control of the company. This is a pretty bold move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:23.220
given that their entire fortunes depend on the&nbsp;
performance of Google. If you ask them directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.840
they’ll tell you that it was time to assume the&nbsp;
role of proud parents - offering advice and love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.140
but not daily nagging. But, if you take a closer&nbsp;
look, you’ll see that there’s more to this than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.360
they let on. It’s not that Larry and Sergey&nbsp;
don’t have visions for Google or that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.460
don’t want to lead the company. Rather, they’ve&nbsp;
found that everytime they try to take the reigns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.880
of the company, shareholders aren’t particularly&nbsp;
happy. And this wasn’t a one or two time event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.400
it was a consistent theme throughout their&nbsp;
entire time at Google. It seems that eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.340
they came to accept that the goals of Google&nbsp;
and their own goals simply did not align. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.380
instead of trying to resist, they did&nbsp;
the classy thing and truly retired. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.480
here’s the truth behind why Google’s founders&nbsp;
really ditched Google and never looked back.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.080
FIRST TRY AS CEO:&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.360
While Larry and Sergey didn’t leave till recent&nbsp;
years, their unorthodox leadership style was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.640
evident from day one. Likely one of the best&nbsp;
examples of this was Larry’s workplace rulebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.180
which consisted of five rules. Rule #1 was Don’t&nbsp;
delegate: do everything you can yourself to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:39.120
things go faster. This is quite ironic given that&nbsp;
you could argue that Larry himself is breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.400
this rule today. Putting that aside though,&nbsp;
effective delegation within any business is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.800
extremely important, but Larry was actively&nbsp;
encouraging employees to specifically avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:56.280
delegation. Moving onto rule #2, we have don't&nbsp;
get in the way if you're not adding value. Let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.020
the people actually doing the work talk to each&nbsp;
other while you go do something else. Rule #2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:07.620
was Don’t be a bureaucrat. Rule #4 was Ideas are&nbsp;
more important than age. Just because someone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:13.920
junior doesn't mean they don't deserve respect&nbsp;
and cooperation. And finally, Rule #5 was The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:19.560
worst thing you can do is stop someone from doing&nbsp;
something by saying, “No. Period.” If you say no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.600
you have to help them find a better way to get&nbsp;
it done. While many of these rules do make sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.900
Larry’s motivation for these rules weren’t exactly&nbsp;
pure. You see, Larry wasn’t trying to create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.120
harmony between managers and engineers. Rather,&nbsp;
he was trying to completely eliminate managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:42.480
altogether because he didn’t like the idea of&nbsp;
non-engineers supervising engineers. He felt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.620
this supervision was not only unnecessary but also&nbsp;
an impediment. Larry was extremely confident in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.720
the quality of his engineering hires, and he felt&nbsp;
that there was no reason for them to be babysit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.520
hence the rule book. Larry was hoping that the&nbsp;
rule book would keep managers out of the hairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:06.060
of engineers. But, with time, he came to realize&nbsp;
that this wasn’t the case and he became more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.220
more irritated. One of the factors that pushed him&nbsp;
over the edge was the fact that he wasn’t seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.900
any progress on his book searching idea. Larry&nbsp;
wanted Google to be able to scan all the books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.580
in the world and then make them searchable&nbsp;
online, you’re probably familiar with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:27.180
feature today. But, this wasn’t going well, and I&nbsp;
don’t think you’d be surprised that he blamed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:31.680
project managers. Instead of holding a meeting&nbsp;
with the managers like a reasonable CEO though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.020
he would hold a townhall style meeting with all&nbsp;
the managers and engineers in July of 2001. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.600
would go on to insult all of the managers in&nbsp;
front of everyone before firing them all. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.480
Larry’s surprise though, this didn’t get anyone&nbsp;
fired except himself. As you guess, the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:54.000
managers were not happy with the choice for&nbsp;
obvious reasons. But, it wasn’t just the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.160
managers that were unhappy either. Many engineers&nbsp;
weren’t convinced by this move either. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.780
engineer even yelled at Larry declaring that&nbsp;
what he was doing was completely ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.520
and totally unprofessional. Google’s&nbsp;
human resources boss, Stacy Sullivan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.720
would escalate this issue to the chairman of&nbsp;
Google, Eric Schmidt, who had been placed there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:15.300
by Google’s investors. Eric would of course&nbsp;
side with the managers and this resulted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:20.820
most of the managers being reinstated and Larry&nbsp;
being demoted from being CEO. Eric would take on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:27.360
this role so that he could supervise Larry and&nbsp;
Sergey. Let’s just say Larry was very unhappy.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.380
BABYSITTING:&nbsp;

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.060
Fortunately for Eric, he didn’t have to do too&nbsp;
much babysitting over the next several years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.520
because Larry and Sergey didn’t poke around too&nbsp;
much when it came to the operations of Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:47.400
Instead, they focused acquisitions which they&nbsp;
knocked out of the park. First, they had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:53.760
Android acquisition in 2005 which cost Google $50&nbsp;
million. Larry actually completed this acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:06:00.540
without even telling Eric, but he didn’t mind.&nbsp;
From Eric’s perspective, Google was already a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:06.780
$100 billion company and if a mere $50 million&nbsp;
could keep Larry occupied and prevent him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:12.120
making any more rash decisions at Google, that&nbsp;
was a major win, and that’s exactly what happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:17.220
Over the next few years, Larry and Sergey would&nbsp;
be enthralled with making a bunch acquisitions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:22.080
And to their credit, many of their acquisitions&nbsp;
proved to be extremely successful like YouTube.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:28.140
Aside from acquisitions, these two were also super&nbsp;
busy with what they like to call the moonshot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.520
factory. Likely the two most famous inventions&nbsp;
out of this factory are of course the Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:37.980
self driving car and Google Glass. Pretty soon,&nbsp;
Eric began to feel that he could trust these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:43.080
once again. Not only were they making phenomenal&nbsp;
acquisitions and ground breaking innovations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:48.300
but they weren’t doing anything that was too&nbsp;
questionable either. They weren’t insulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:52.980
employees infront of each other nor were they&nbsp;
firing every manager in the company. It seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:59.100
like the duo had matured as business leaders,&nbsp;
and shareholders very much agreed. So, in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:04.920
when Eric stepped down as CEO, he would pass on&nbsp;
the title to Larry. But, it didn’t take long for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:10.200
everyone to realize that nothing had changed for&nbsp;
Larry or Sergey. They had simply found an outlook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:15.600
for their unorthodox ideas with acquisitions&nbsp;
and the moonshot factory. But, now that Larry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.340
was back to running Google proper, many of the&nbsp;
same issues started to resurface once again.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.740
SECOND TRY AS CEO:
A common theme for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:31.440
Larry seems to be that he simply doesn’t like&nbsp;
oversight in general. Originally, he didn’t like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:37.140
managers overseeing engineers, but that was before&nbsp;
Google went public. Now that Google was public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:43.320
one of his largest concerns was investor oversight&nbsp;
of Google, but he wasn’t gonna immediately try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:48.240
tackle that issue. The reason that he got screwed&nbsp;
a decade ago was because the employees weren’t on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.400
his side. So, this time he was going to make&nbsp;
sure that investors were on his side before he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.380
tried anything too radical. And this started&nbsp;
with giving investors what they wanted most:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.060
sustainable growth. Larry started off&nbsp;
by slashing Google’s products, features,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:09.300
and services. Apparently, he reduced these by&nbsp;
70 within just 2 years of taking over. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.860
placed an emphasis on design and simplicity trying&nbsp;
to emulate some of Apple’s success within that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.840
realm. And finally, Larry also implemented&nbsp;
a new constraint for acquisitions called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:23.760
the toothbrush test. Instead of looking at the&nbsp;
profitability or financials of a given company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:28.320
Google evaluated these companies based&nbsp;
on their usefulness alone. If a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.820
offers something that people will use once or&nbsp;
twice per day and it makes their life better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:38.640
the company is a winner. If it doesn’t do that,&nbsp;
however, Larry wouldn’t buy the company even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.260
they have tremendous financials. While this&nbsp;
was defintely on the side of unorthodox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:48.720
it worked. Google’s revenue was growing, their&nbsp;
profits were growing, and most importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:54.480
their stock price was growing. Investors were&nbsp;
starting to trust Larry, but it didn’t take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.760
very long for him to take things too far once&nbsp;
again with Google Island. If you haven’t heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.500
about Google Island, it was essentially Larry’s&nbsp;
idea to take Google off the grid not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:10.080
physically but metaphorically. He wanted Google&nbsp;
to have freedom from not only investors but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.940
government regulation. One of his proposed ideas&nbsp;
was that laws older than 50 years wouldn’t apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:19.920
to tech companies. TechCrunch put it as Larry&nbsp;
wanting the Earth to have a mad scientist island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:26.340
that was run by Google. While this may sound&nbsp;
cool in theory, it was also super unrealistic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:31.440
Google Island would not only free Google from&nbsp;
some of the barriers of innovation but it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.780
also free them from accountability altogether. If&nbsp;
neither the government nor investors could keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:42.180
Google accountable, who would? Obviously, Larry&nbsp;
believed that he could keep Google accountable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:48.420
but this was more of a pipe dream than anything.&nbsp;
After pitching this idea, dozens of publications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:54.540
would ruthlessly bag on Larry claiming that he was&nbsp;
completely out of touch from reality. And almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:59.880
all of them posed the same question: is this the&nbsp;
type of guy you wanted to be in charge of Google?

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:03.960
A REALIZATION:&nbsp;

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:09.420
No one would fire Larry from his position the&nbsp;
second time around, but Larry himself had come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:15.360
to a realization: Google would never be what he&nbsp;
wanted it to be. This isn’t to say that Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.700
wouldn’t be successful or that it wouldn’t make a&nbsp;
boatload of money because it’s done both of those.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:25.920
But, what this did mean though is that Google&nbsp;
would never become a groundbreaking research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:33.180
institution which is what Larry and Sergey&nbsp;
wanted from day 1. Larry and Sergey were never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.920
your traditional tech founders. They weren’t your&nbsp;
classic college dropouts who wanted to change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.620
world and became megarich. They were actually PhD&nbsp;
students who founded Google as a research project.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:48.840
And while they were all for the financial side of&nbsp;
Google, they didn’t want for Google to lose its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:54.300
identity as a research institution either.&nbsp;
But with time, it was clear that investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:59.340
didn’t really care about this unless it could&nbsp;
directly be linked to profits. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:04.980
both Larry and Sergey were both facing significant&nbsp;
personal challenges as well. Larry had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:09.660
struggling with vocal chord paralysis for much of&nbsp;
his adult life and things were only getting worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:14.640
in the early 2010s. This made it especially&nbsp;
difficult for Larry to engage in interviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.220
earnings calls, and other PR events which are&nbsp;
extremely important for any public company CEO.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:26.820
This would make Larry infamous for being MIA.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, Sergey had gotten caught up in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:31.980
affair which not only ruined his public reputation&nbsp;
but also his relationship with Larry who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:37.260
refused to speak to him since. Given that Larry&nbsp;
and Sergey have 51% voting power at Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:42.840
they could technically take Google in whatever&nbsp;
direction they want. But, given that they only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:48.120
own about 14% of the company together, I think it&nbsp;
was pretty clear to them that abusing their voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:53.880
power would be a pretty douchy thing to do to the&nbsp;
other 86% of shareholders. So, they would decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:58.020
to do the classy thing and give shareholders&nbsp;
what they wanted. They gave the shareholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:03.960
a CEO that could grow Google into a trillion&nbsp;
dollar company: Sundar Pichai. In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:08.460
Larry and Sergey have ventured off into&nbsp;
what they’ve always wanted to do. Research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:14.640
exciting ideas like flying cars and anti aging and&nbsp;
hopefully bring them to fruition. And that’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.680
Google’s founders really left Google. Do you think&nbsp;
Google would be better under Larry and Sergey’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.320
leadership? Comment that down below. Also, drop a&nbsp;
like if you hope that Google doesn't forget their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:30.600
slogan: Don’t be Evil. And of course, consider&nbsp;
